<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>BlazorNative.Testing</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Same two gates every shipped package carries: the public-API baseline
         (RS0016/17/37 as ERRORS) and doc coverage (CS1591 as an ERROR). Both
         switches live in src/Directory.Build.targets, not .props — see the Device
         csproj's note for why that ordering is load-bearing. -->
    <BnEnforcePublicApi>true</BnEnforcePublicApi>
    <BnEnforceDocCoverage>true</BnEnforceDocCoverage>

    <!-- BL0006: "do not use internal Blazor render-tree types". Suppressed
         project-wide, exactly as BlazorNative.Renderer does and for the same
         reason: every touch here goes through NativeRenderer — mounting,
         dispatching an event, disposing — and NativeRenderer derives from Blazor's
         Renderer, so the warning fires on the package's whole purpose rather than
         on a slip. ABSORBING that is what this package is FOR: the internals stop
         here so a consumer's test project never sees them. (Scoped pragmas were
         tried first on the theory that there was one call site; there are several,
         and a pragma per member would be noise claiming to be precision.) -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);BL0006</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         THE EIGHTH PACKAGE, and it is the first one added against the
         "no 8th package" precedent — so the reason is here, not only in the
         design doc (docs/plans/2026-08-18-consumer-test-harness-design.md §3).

         That precedent is real and recorded four times: phases 9.1/9.2/9.3 each
         added a CAPABILITY to an existing package rather than splitting one off,
         and PackagePurityTests pins the shipped set. It does not apply here.
         Those decisions were about not fragmenting the capability surface —
         IGeolocation belongs beside ICamera. This is not a capability.

         The positive case decides it: THIS IS A DEV-TIME-ONLY DEPENDENCY. An app
         references it from its TEST project and it must never enter the app's own
         reference graph. The alternative — putting the harness beside
         DevHostBridge in BlazorNative.Core — would ship test-double machinery
         into every production binary and freeze it as part of Core's public API
         at 1.0. A test harness is exactly the surface that should stay free to
         move for longer than the runtime does.

         And the pin's own words are "nothing else under src/ may grow a csproj
         WITHOUT JOINING THIS PIN" — a process, deliberately joined here.

         TIER: PROVISIONAL at introduction, for the same reason DevHostBridge is —
         the second end of the contract (real consumer test suites) does not exist
         yet, so the shape is honest today and may be wrong in one release.
         ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <Description>BlazorNative test harness: mount a page in-process and assert the widget tree it rendered — children in real sibling order, styles, props and events — then drive clicks and changes. The supported alternative to reaching into NativeRenderer, which is not public API.</Description>
    <PackageTags>blazor;native;testing;unit-testing;xunit;nativeaot;android;ios;mobile</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Renderer, because the harness consumes RenderFrame/RenderPatch to build
         the projection. That dependency is the POINT of the package: it absorbs
         the NOT-API model here, once, so a consumer never takes it. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\BlazorNative.Renderer\BlazorNative.Renderer.csproj" />
    <!-- BN rules ride every src build (zero-warning bar, M4 DoD #3). -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\BlazorNative.Analyzers\BlazorNative.Analyzers.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
